-*   **Boundary Conditions:** The slice parameter `i / width` assumes a specific mapping between the normalized value and the volume coordinate system. Incorrect mapping may result in missing or duplicated slices [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
+### [Voxel Generation via 2D Slice Texture](15471-voxel-generation-via-2d-slice-texture.md)
+This method generates billions of explicit voxels without using 3D textures or compression by treating a single large 2D texture as a column of 2D slices. It solves the memory bottleneck of storing full 3D volumes by rendering voxel data to a bitmap (chunk/page) and discarding it immediately after use. It differs from sibling methods by explicitly generating every voxel in a shader rather than sampling implicit data, and by reusing a single 2D texture buffer for multiple chunks to minimize VRAM allocation.
+
 ### 3D Texture Generation via Vertex Shader Iteration
-This method renders to a specific slice of a 3D texture in a single draw call by iterating over texels of a source depth buffer in the vertex shader and projecting them into the 3D volume [S3, unofficial]. It solves the performance penalty of multiple draw calls (one per slice) and redundant sampling of the depth texture for every slice [S3, unofficial]. It differs from the primary slicing method by avoiding the CPU-GPU round-trip for each slice and by using `gl_Layer` to target slices directly [S3, unofficial].
-
-**Steps:**
-1.  Draw points, with the number of points equal to the depth buffer resolution or less [S3, unofficial].
-2.  In the vertex shader, fetch the depth buffer value for the current point [S3, unofficial].
-3.  Project the fetched depth value into the cube covered by the 3D texture to determine the Z-coordinate (slice) [S3, unofficial].
-4.  Set the target slice for rendering using the `gl_Layer` variable in the vertex shader [S3, unofficial].
-5.  In the fragment shader, perform the desired calculations (e.g., processing color textures) and write to the 3D texture slice [S3, unofficial].
-
-**Parameters:**
-*   `gl_Layer`: A built-in variable used to specify the layer/slice index for rendering to a 3D texture or array texture [S3, unofficial].
-
-**Pitfalls:**
-*   The `gl_Layer` variable in the vertex shader is not part of the core OpenGL specification prior to version 4.2; it requires the `GL_AMD_vertex_shader_layer` extension or a geometry shader pass-through [S3, unofficial].
+This method renders to a specific slice of a 3D texture in a single draw call by iterating over texels of a source depth buffer in the vertex shader and projecting them into the 3D volume [S5, unofficial]. It solves the performance penalty of multiple draw calls (one per slice) and redundant sampling of the depth texture for every slice [S5, unofficial]. It differs from the primary slicing method by avoiding the CPU-GPU round-trip for each slice and by using `gl_Layer` to target slices directly [S5, unofficial]. Steps: draw points equal to the depth buffer resolution; fetch the depth value in the vertex shader; project the depth into the cube to determine the Z-coordinate; set the target slice via `gl_Layer`; process in the fragment shader [S5, unofficial]. Pitfall: `gl_Layer` in the vertex shader requires OpenGL 4.2 core or the `GL_AMD_vertex_shader_layer` extension [S5, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N^3)$ for a volume of size $N \times N \times N$, due to $N$ draw calls and $N^2$ pixels per slice [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N^3)$ for storing the volume data [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Measured Performance:** Not given in the sources. However, it is noted as "terribly slow" compared to single-draw-call methods when dealing with large volumes due to the overhead of multiple draw calls [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Scaling:** Scales poorly with volume size due to the linear increase in draw calls [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Simple to implement; described as one of the simplest methods for volume visualization [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** High CPU overhead due to multiple draw calls; slow pixel readback from GPU to CPU [S2, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** Small volumes or prototyping where simplicity is prioritized over performance [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When not to use:** Large volumes or real-time applications requiring high performance [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ draw calls for $N$ slices, with $O(N^3)$ total pixel processing [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N^3)$ for the volume data, reduced to 1 byte per voxel using `GL_RED` format (e.g., 256 MB for a 1024×1024×256 grid) [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Measured Performance:** Direct GPU copy via `glCopyTexSubImage3D` is efficient; CPU readback is slow and requires PBOs to mitigate overhead [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Scaling:** Scales linearly with slice count; VRAM usage scales with volume resolution [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Simple implementation; direct render-to-3D-texture pipeline; automatic mipmap generation support [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** High VRAM if storing multi-component data (e.g., normals); concave mesh handling; potential noise artifacts [S1, S4, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** Prototyping, simple volume visualization, or when direct GPU voxelization is required [S1, S2, unofficial].
+*   **When not to use:** Large-scale real-time applications requiring multi-component per-voxel data or complex concave geometry without post-processing [S1, S4, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-This method is described as one of the simplest methods for volume visualization [S1, unofficial]. It is commonly used in educational contexts and simple volume renderers [S2, unofficial]. The vertex shader iteration variant was discussed in a 2013 Stack Exchange post as an optimization for rendering to 3D textures [S3, unofficial].
+The technique originated from a 2008 forum discussion on Ken Silverman's Real-time voxel raytracing development thread, where developers compared framebuffer-based voxelization against stencil-buffer approaches [S1, unofficial]. It is described as one of the simplest methods for volume visualization [S2, unofficial]. The method is used in voxel raycasting implementations and educational volume renderers [S1, S2, unofficial].
 
